POLICY CALL RULED 2026-09-05 (seat16, row unimplemented-feature-gaps-ord-and-dump), both behaviours measured side by side as required before touching code: SCRIP fails ORD('A') at PARSE time ("Error 5 -- Undefined function or operation", core_err_msgs[5], never reaches runtime); the oracle fails it at RUNTIME ("ERROR 022 -- undefined function called"), then degrades softly through its normal end-of-program stats footer, rc=0. Neither tool computes a real ordinal value -- ORD is unimplemented in BOTH, not a SCRIP-only gap. Matching the oracle byte-for-byte is NOT a scoped fix for this row: it would require (a) SCRIP to stop treating an unrecognized function-call identifier as a parse-time fatal and defer to a runtime "undefined function" error instead -- a general SNOBOL4 semantics change (function-call resolution is inherently runtime in this language), not an ORD-specific patch, and (b) SCRIP to print SPITBOL's OWN error number for that condition, which it currently cannot: FINDING-2026-09-05-seat08-core_err_msgs-is-not-indexed-by-official-spitbol-error-numbers.md already proved core_err_msgs[5]'s "Undefined function or operation" is SCRIP's internal code 5, not SPITBOL's official 22, and that FINDING explicitly scopes the general fix (transcribe the manual's full 61+-entry table, audit every hardcoded core_runtime_error/kwb_error call site) as its OWN cross-cutting row given real regression risk -- riding this row's fix on top of that unlanded row is out of scope, not merely deferred out of laziness. RULING: ORD stays out of SCRIP's implemented-function set; likely a CSNOBOL4-only extension outside SPITBOL-semantics target, and not closeable standalone regardless of that classification, since even a correct implementation would still need the error-numbering fix to byte-match. Class row `unimplemented-feature-gaps-ord-and-dump` (minted, hq_T) -- policy question closed, code fix blocked on a separate, larger, already-routed row.
